<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:content="http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/"><channel><title>Universal-Codes on metafunctor</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/tags/universal-codes/</link><description>Research Engineer &amp;amp; Computer Scientist - Machine Learning, Statistical Computing, Open Source Development</description><language>en-us</language><managingEditor>lex@metafunctor.com (Alex Towell)</managingEditor><webMaster>lex@metafunctor.com (Alex Towell)</webMaster><lastBuildDate>Thu, 09 Jul 2026 08:44:23 -0500</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://queelius.github.io/tags/universal-codes/" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><image><url>https://queelius.github.io/images/og-image.jpg</url><title>metafunctor</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/</link></image><item><title>Synthesis: Codecs as Structure</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-05-synthesis-wire-formats/</link><pubDate>Fri, 15 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-05-synthesis-wire-formats/</guid><description>The series closes by restating the codes-as-priors thesis across all twelve instances and connecting the wire-format side to the Stepanov type-algebra side.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-05-synthesis-wire-formats/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Universal-Codes</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>information-theory</category><category>coding-theory</category><category>prefix-free</category><category>universal-codes</category><category>synthesis</category><category>algebra</category></item><item><title>PFC: Zero-Copy Data Compression Through Prefix-Free Codecs</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-06-pfc/</link><pubDate>Mon, 10 Jun 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-06-pfc/</guid><description>A header-only C++20 library that achieves 3-10x compression with zero marshaling overhead using prefix-free codes and Stepanov-style generic programming.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-06-pfc/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Universal-Codes</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>compression</category><category>prefix-free-codes</category><category>zero-copy</category><category>generic-programming</category><category>universal-codes</category><category>STL</category><category>template-metaprogramming</category><category>Elias-codes</category></item><item><title>VByte / Varint</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-02-vbyte-wire-formats/</link><pubDate>Sun, 25 Feb 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-02-vbyte-wire-formats/</guid><description>VByte trades bit-level precision for byte-alignment, and that trade wins in practice. Most production columnar databases and network protocols use VByte for integer encoding.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-02-vbyte-wire-formats/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Universal-Codes</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>information-theory</category><category>coding-theory</category><category>prefix-free</category><category>universal-codes</category><category>vbyte</category><category>varint</category><category>byte-aligned</category></item><item><title>Rice / Golomb</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2023-09-rice-golomb-wire-formats/</link><pubDate>Sun, 17 Sep 2023 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2023-09-rice-golomb-wire-formats/</guid><description>Rice and Golomb codes are parametric: a single parameter k (or m) tunes the code to a specific geometric distribution. Choosing k is choosing your prior precisely.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2023-09-rice-golomb-wire-formats/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Universal-Codes</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>information-theory</category><category>coding-theory</category><category>prefix-free</category><category>universal-codes</category><category>rice</category><category>golomb</category><category>geometric-distribution</category></item><item><title>Fibonacci Coding</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2023-04-fibonacci-wire-formats/</link><pubDate>Sun, 23 Apr 2023 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2023-04-fibonacci-wire-formats/</guid><description>Fibonacci coding uses Zeckendorf&amp;#39;s representation to produce self-synchronizing codewords. Every codeword ends in two consecutive ones; a single bit flip corrupts at most two codewords.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2023-04-fibonacci-wire-formats/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Universal-Codes</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>information-theory</category><category>coding-theory</category><category>prefix-free</category><category>universal-codes</category><category>fibonacci</category><category>zeckendorf</category></item><item><title>Elias Delta and Omega</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2022-11-elias-delta-omega-wire-formats/</link><pubDate>Sun, 13 Nov 2022 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2022-11-elias-delta-omega-wire-formats/</guid><description>Elias delta and omega extend Elias gamma by recursively encoding the length prefix. Each step yields shorter codewords for large integers at a small constant cost for small ones.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2022-11-elias-delta-omega-wire-formats/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Universal-Codes</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>information-theory</category><category>coding-theory</category><category>prefix-free</category><category>universal-codes</category><category>elias-delta</category><category>elias-omega</category></item><item><title>Unary and Elias Gamma</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2022-06-elias-gamma-wire-formats/</link><pubDate>Sun, 19 Jun 2022 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2022-06-elias-gamma-wire-formats/</guid><description>Unary and Elias gamma are the two simplest universal codes. Unary encodes n in n bits; gamma in 2 log2(n)+1 bits. Each implies a different prior over the integers.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2022-06-elias-gamma-wire-formats/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Universal-Codes</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>information-theory</category><category>coding-theory</category><category>prefix-free</category><category>universal-codes</category><category>unary</category><category>elias-gamma</category></item><item><title>Universal Codes as Priors</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2022-01-priors-wire-formats/</link><pubDate>Sat, 15 Jan 2022 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2022-01-priors-wire-formats/</guid><description>Every prefix-free code is a hypothesis about the source. The codeword lengths determine an implicit probability distribution; the code is optimal when that prior matches the true source.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2022-01-priors-wire-formats/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Universal-Codes</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>information-theory</category><category>coding-theory</category><category>prefix-free</category><category>universal-codes</category><category>Shannon</category></item></channel></rss>